          "text": "{\"category\":\"global waveform-feature distribution shift\",\"cause\":\"unknown; leading hypotheses are a run-wide digitizer/pulse-finding configuration mismatch or an AutoDQM reference/preprocessing mismatch, with broad digitizer data corruption less likely\",\"action\":\"First compare raw waveforms and per-channel feature distributions against a known-good run, and verify the active digitizer thresholds, firmware, channel map, and pulse-finding configuration. Recompute the anomaly scores with a validated matching reference and inspect whether the million-scale z-scores arise from zero or near-zero reference variance. If raw waveforms are abnormal across many channels despite correct configuration, check digitizer synchronization, board matching, queue occupancy, and per-board rates; restart or reconfigure only the implicated DAQ component. Do not pursue PMT-base or LVDS replacement unless HV/current logs, raw waveforms, or connection tests localize the fault to specific paired channels.\",\"confidence\":0.48,\"supporting_historical_runs\":[1605,1642],\"reasoning_summary\":\"OBSERVED: all 627 subruns are anomalous, with 52,957 channel-entries and extreme z-scores across pulse-count, height, area, and duration features. Trigger rate and LVDS totals are persistent; no LVDS pin is persistently zero, missing, low, or high. No missing detector channels, HV telemetry, board matching, digitizer-rate consistency, synchronization, queue, or within-run configuration data are supplied. INFERRED: the breadth and persistence favor a global feature/configuration/reference shift over a localized PMT-base or LVDS fault. Runs 1605 and 1642 provide only partial similarity through million-scale pulse-feature z-scores; there is no exact historical match, and 1642 additionally had missing channels tied to a known PMT-base problem, which is absent here. A DAQ corruption hypothesis remains possible but is weakened by stable trigger/LVDS summaries and cannot be tested without board-level telemetry. Discriminating checks are raw-waveform comparison, active-versus-expected configuration verification, reference-variance validation, and board matching/rate telemetry.\"}"
        }
